{"name":"Ripening Grape","hex":"#6f3942","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"ripening","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The process of becoming ripe."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["present participle and gerund of ripen"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ripening"},{"term":"grape","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A small, round, smooth-skinned edible fruit, usually purple, red, or green, that grows in bunches on female vines of most species of genus Vitis and the self-fertile plants of species Vitis vinifera.","Any woody vine of genus Vitis that bears clusters of grapes; a grapevine."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Containing grapes or having a grape flavor.","Of a dark purplish red colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/grape"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ripeness in viticulture","blocks":[{"text":"In viticulture, ripeness is the completion of the ripening process of wine grapes on the vine which signals the beginning of harvest. What exactly constitutes ripeness will vary depending on what style of wine is being produced and what the winemaker and viticulturist personally believe constitutes ripeness. Once the grapes are harvested, the physical and chemical components of the grape which will influence a wine's quality are essentially set so determining the optimal moment of ripeness for harvest may be considered the most crucial decision in winemaking."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/Wine_grapes07.jpg/330px-Wine_grapes07.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ripeness_in_viticulture"},{"title":"Ripening","blocks":[{"text":"Ripening is a process in fruits that causes them to become more palatable. In general, fruit becomes sweeter, less green, and softer as it ripens. Even though the acidity of fruit increases as it ripens, the higher acidity level does not make the fruit seem tarter. This effect is attributed to the Brix-Acid Ratio. Climacteric fruits ripen after harvesting and so some fruits for market are picked green."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Veraison_Cabernet_Sauvignon.jpg/330px-Veraison_Cabernet_Sauvignon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ripening"},{"title":"Grape","blocks":[{"text":"A grape is a fruit, botanically a berry, of the deciduous woody vines of the flowering plant genus Vitis. Grapes are a non-climacteric type of fruit, generally occurring in clusters."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Grapes%2C_Rostov-on-Don%2C_Russia.jpg/330px-Grapes%2C_Rostov-on-Don%2C_Russia.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grape"},{"title":"Shades of violet","blocks":[{"text":"Violet is a color term derived from the flower of the same name. There are numerous variations of the color violet, a sampling of which are shown below."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Color_icon_violet_v3.svg/330px-Color_icon_violet_v3.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shades_of_violet"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Pinot_Noir_Grapes_-_Cristom_Vineyard_-_Oregon_in_early_veraison.jpg/500px-Pinot_Noir_Grapes_-_Cristom_Vineyard_-_Oregon_in_early_veraison.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Pinot_Noir_Grapes_-_Cristom_Vineyard_-_Oregon_in_early_veraison.jpg/1280px-Pinot_Noir_Grapes_-_Cristom_Vineyard_-_Oregon_in_early_veraison.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Pinot_Noir_Grapes_-_Cristom_Vineyard_-_Oregon_in_early_veraison.jpg/500px-Pinot_Noir_Grapes_-_Cristom_Vineyard_-_Oregon_in_early_veraison.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Pinot noir grapes in the early stages of veraison. As the grapes ripen, the concentration of phenolic compounds like anthocyanins replaces the green color of chlorophyll in the grape berries which makes them black instead.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APinot_Noir_Grapes_-_Cristom_Vineyard_-_Oregon_in_early_veraison.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Pinot_Noir_grapes_after_veraison.jpg/500px-Pinot_Noir_grapes_after_veraison.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Pinot_Noir_grapes_after_veraison.jpg/1280px-Pinot_Noir_grapes_after_veraison.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Pinot_Noir_grapes_after_veraison.jpg/500px-Pinot_Noir_grapes_after_veraison.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Pinot noir grapes that are destined for sparkling wine will be considered ripe much earlier than Pinot noir destined for still red wine.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APinot_Noir_grapes_after_veraison.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Grape-vine.JPG/500px-Grape-vine.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Grape-vine.JPG/1280px-Grape-vine.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Grape-vine.JPG/500px-Grape-vine.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Vineyard management techniques such as canopy management can influence the ripening process of grapes by balancing the amount of foliage needed for photosynthesis versus excessive foliage that shades the grapes and competes for the grapevine's resources.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGrape-vine.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/Very_ripe%2C_dehydrated_grapes.jpg/500px-Very_ripe%2C_dehydrated_grapes.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/Very_ripe%2C_dehydrated_grapes.jpg/1280px-Very_ripe%2C_dehydrated_grapes.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/Very_ripe%2C_dehydrated_grapes.jpg/500px-Very_ripe%2C_dehydrated_grapes.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Grapes that have been left on the vine too long may become over ripe and dehydrated.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVery_ripe%2C_dehydrated_grapes.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/PH_scale_with_wine_highlighted.jpg/500px-PH_scale_with_wine_highlighted.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/PH_scale_with_wine_highlighted.jpg/500px-PH_scale_with_wine_highlighted.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The pH levels for most wine fall between 3 and 4 on the pH scale.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APH_scale_with_wine_highlighted.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/WinzerMitRefraktometer.jpg/500px-WinzerMitRefraktometer.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/WinzerMitRefraktometer.jpg/1280px-WinzerMitRefraktometer.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/WinzerMitRefraktometer.jpg/500px-WinzerMitRefraktometer.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Winemakers use a refractometer on samples of grapes picked in the vineyard to measure sugar levels while determining ripeness.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWinzerMitRefraktometer.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Grapes_with_green_stems.jpg/500px-Grapes_with_green_stems.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Grapes_with_green_stems.jpg/1280px-Grapes_with_green_stems.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Grapes_with_green_stems.jpg/500px-Grapes_with_green_stems.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"In determining physiological ripeness, winemakers will observe the lignification of the grape stems as they turn from being flexible and green to hard, woody and brown.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGrapes_with_green_stems.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/Grapes_nearing_ripeness.jpg/500px-Grapes_nearing_ripeness.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/Grapes_nearing_ripeness.jpg/1280px-Grapes_nearing_ripeness.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/Grapes_nearing_ripeness.jpg/500px-Grapes_nearing_ripeness.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Researchers in the wine industry are developing new ways to objectively measure ripeness.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGrapes_nearing_ripeness.jpg"}],"sounds":{"grape":[{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/62/En-uk-grape.ogg?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/6/62/En-uk-grape.ogg/En-uk-grape.ogg.mp3"},{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0f/En-us-grape.ogg?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/0/0f/En-us-grape.ogg/En-us-grape.ogg.mp3"}]}}}